{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And to rule over the day and over the night.</b> The sun rules the day and the moon rules the night [and the day]. Know that besides the excellence of light that they were privileged to attain, He gave them dominion over the lower beings, in coming-into-being and in perishing — for the sun, through that dominion it has by day, causes growth and generation and increase in all hot and dry things; and the moon likewise, through that dominion it has by night, increases the springs in the seas and in the streams and in all cold and moist things." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, ולמשול ביום ובלילה alludes to the governors of the two great luminaries, who rule by day and by night, and through their power every ruler rules. Corresponding to them you find in the daily offering: שני כבשים בני שנה תמימים את הכבש אחד תעשה בבקר ואת הכבש השני תעשה בין הערבים — and this is what the verse says (Deuteronomy 4:19): אשר חלק ה' אלהיך אותם לכל העמים, that is to say, the sun and the moon and all the host of heaven, ואתכם לקח ה'. And the discerning one will understand. And this is what is written (Deuteronomy 17:3): אשר לא צויתי, that is to say, concerning the sun and the moon — but I did command concerning the governors. And in the portion Shofetim, at the verse (Deuteronomy 17:3) ולשמש או לירח או לכל צבא השמים אשר לא צויתי, I shall write of this, with the help of Heaven. From here it will become clear to you the secret of the fast, for it too is an offering, since the flesh and fat and blood of a person are diminished. Therefore the fast requires acceptance during the time of the joint dominion of the two luminaries, and this is מבעוד יום, before the sun sets — and this is the statement of Rav Ḥisda: כל תענית שלא קבלו עליו מבעוד יום לא שמיה תענית. And Rav Ḥisda said: כל תענית שלא שקעה עליו חמה לא שמיה תענית. And just as the fast is an offering, so too the tearshedding at the time of prayer is an offering, corresponding to the water libation with the offerings. And so the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, expounded: the gates of tears are not locked, for tears are from the power of water, which is from the power of Ḥesed, which is the right side, and the right side is open to receive those who return at all times and at every hour. And understand this, for it is a great principle." "<b>And to divide between the light and between the darkness.</b> Here He mentioned three matters — to give light, and to rule, and to divide — for the Holy One, blessed be He, grants them excellence in these three things. And it is known that He, blessed be He, empowers some of His works over others; and of this the verse says (Isaiah 55:10): ונתן זרע לזורע ולחם לאוכל — and the rain does not give the seed and the bread, but the verse teaches you that the Holy One, blessed be He, empowers some of His works over others, in the manner of what is written (Hosea 2:23): אענה נאם ה' אענה את השמים והם יענו את הארץ והארץ תענה את הדגן ואת התירוש ואת היצהר. This verse intends to say that although the heavens and the earth produce what is in their power and what was appointed under their hand, their power is not from themselves but from the power of the supernal Cause, for all of them are emissaries from Him to act upon the lower beings according to His will, not according to their will. And this is what is mentioned here — להאיר ולמשול ולהבדיל — that is to say, He imposed upon them this appointment and power, and delivered it into their hands; not that He, blessed be He, is divested of His power, which is supernal above them. For had He said to them, 'give light' and 'divide' on their own, He would have delivered the power into their hands entirely, and would have made them deities, and there would be found in them an opening for idolators. And the warning has already come in the Torah that a person not go astray after idolatry — that is His saying (Deuteronomy 4:19): ופן תשא עיניך השמימה וראית את השמש ואת הירח ואת הכוכבים כל צבא השמים ותתבונן בהם ומקוריהם שהם שואבים מהם והשתחוית להם ועבדתם אשר חלק ה' אלהיך אותם לכל העמים תחת כל השמים." "<b>And you already</b> know that the sages of Israel needed to write to King Ptolemy אשר חלק להאיר, so that he not say that an opening for idolatry was found for them from this, and say that from this verse the verse permitted idolators to worship idols, since it is written explicitly in the Torah that the Holy One, blessed be He, gave them as the portion of the nations — and he would understand the word חלק as meaning 'decreed.' But the matter is not so; rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, gave the choice into the hand of each and every nation to worship whomever they wish, whether the Holy One, blessed be He, or an idol — and this is the sense of חלק: He gave them permission. Not so with Israel, for if they wish, Heaven forfend, to worship idols, they cannot — that is what the prophet, peace be upon him, said concerning the Holy One, blessed be He (Ezekiel 20:33): אם לא ביד חזקה ובזרוע נטויה ובחמה שפוכה אמלוך עליכם." "<b>And you should know</b> that the reason the luminaries were created on the fourth day is because the fourth day is the middle of the seven days of Creation, and the sun is fixed in the fourth sphere, which is the middle of the seven spheres that serve, in the order of צצם חנכ״ל. And further, because these two luminaries change every day four times daily — in the four watches, and in the month in four weeks, and in the year in four seasons — therefore the fourth day was singled out for them. This and yet another reason: for it was a necessary matter that the luminaries be created on the fourth day, after the creation of the plants on the third day, in order to teach us thereby the truth of the creation of the world ex nihilo. For had the utterance יהי מאורות been on the third day and the utterance תדשא הארץ on the fourth day, it would have been inferred from this the eternity of the world, for the trees and plants would not have come into the world by the power of the utterance but only by the power of the luminaries already created, which are what bestow power upon all that comes forth from the earth. But now that He preceded תדשא הארץ to the utterance יהי מאורות, this is absolute proof for the creation of the world ex nihilo, for at a time when there were no luminaries in the world to act upon the earth through their motion to generate it and make it sprout, the Holy One, blessed be He, decreed תדשא הארץ, and it brought forth its fruits by the utterance, before there existed the powers of the luminaries, which are their cause — through the power of the supernal Cause, blessed be He." "<b>And know</b> that in this passage you will find the expression מאור five times, because there are five kinds of light: the light of day, the light of the sun, the light of the moon, the light of the stars, the light of the constellations; but none was created to illuminate the earth except the sphere of the sun alone." "<b>And Elohim saw the light, that it was good.</b> The arrangement of the luminaries is a great benefit to creatures, for this lower world is governed by light and darkness. And it is written (Job 9:9): ״עושה עש כסיל וכימה וחדרי תימן״ — Kimah is cold and Kesil is hot; He created this opposite that — this one cools the world and that one heats the world, and both cause the plants to grow and ripen the fruits. And thus it is written (Job 38:31): ״התקשר מעדנות כימה״. And through the order of this activity and the arrangement of the luminaries, human beings see and perceive the power of the deeds of the Almighty, and they praise and exalt Him. And thus it is written (Isaiah 40:26): ״שאו מרום עיניכם וראו מי ברא אלה״ — and this is the goodness, that through seeing this activity they will recognize that they are created beings and will not stray after them. And further, He said 'that it was good' on account of the great miracle that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to perform through the luminaries by the hand of Joshua — this is what is written (Joshua 10:12): ״שמש בגבעון דום וירח בעמק אילון״. And it is written (Judges 5): ״הכוכבים ממסלותם״ etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {6 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {3 "The Hebrew phrase אשר חלק להאיר (which appears to be a reference to the Septuagint rendering of Deuteronomy 4:19) is translated hyper-literally without expanding on what may be an obscure or variant reading.", 4 "The Hebrew reads שני המאורו, apparently a defective spelling of המאורות; rendered as 'the two luminaries.'", 6 "Source text has 'ישעיהו מ׳:כ״ו' — the typographic symbols are editorial markers (page/line indicators), normalized to Isaiah 40:26 per the cited verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:26:19.201851365Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}